# Target C — audited §166 outer atomic endpoint mass versus outer-walk detour

**Date:** 2026-09-12
**Lifecycle:** audited-working proof-only Type-II route module; theorem-state authority unchanged.
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

Section 154 identifies the missing all-regime bridge: atomic stopped-train directions can be paid either by bounded defect-face endpoints or by the occurrence-counted old outer contact walk. Section 166 gives the first quantitative version of that bridge when the outer walk has positive detour.

Put

\[
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3}.
\]

Pass to a Type-II subsequence on which the normalized scalar quantities and the train/outer projective direction measures converge. Write

\[
M:=\lim {b\over D},
\qquad
B:=\lim {Q+4f\over D},
\qquad
C:=\lim {T-q/2\over D},
\tag{166.1}
\]

and, using `per(F)/D->pi`,

\[
\delta:=\lim {b-\operatorname{per}(F)\over D}=M-\pi\ge0.
\tag{166.2}
\]

Then

\[
\boxed{
B+\bigl(3M^2\delta\bigr)^{1/3}
\ge {8\Delta_2\over3C}.
}
\tag{166.3}
\]

Using only `M<=2pi` and `C<=(pi+B)/2`, this implies the fully scalar inequality

\[
\boxed{
B+\bigl(12\pi^2\delta\bigr)^{1/3}
\ge
{16\Delta_2\over3(\pi+B)}
=
{8\pi\over3\sqrt3\,(\pi+B)}.
}
\tag{166.4}
\]

Thus sufficiently small bounded-face endpoint capacity forces a strictly linear outer-walk detour. No zero-detour hypothesis is used.

---

## 166.1 Outer projection excess equals detour

Let

\[
\nu_D={1\over D}\sum_{e\in\partial_{out}C}^{occ}\delta_{[e]}
\Rightarrow\nu,
\]

where bridge and articulation repetitions are retained. Its mass is `M`. Define

\[
F(\alpha)=\int|\cos(\theta-\alpha)|\,d\nu(\theta).
\]

Projection variation of the closed outer walk and the uniform Type-II width limit give

\[
\boxed{F(\alpha)\ge2\quad\forall\alpha.}
\tag{166.5}
\]

Also `F<=M` and

\[
\int_0^\pi F(\alpha)d\alpha=2M.
\]

Hence for `h=F-2>=0`,

\[
\boxed{\int_0^\pi h=2(M-\pi)=2\delta.}
\tag{166.6}
\]

So normalized outer detour is exactly half of the cosine-transform excess above the disk baseline.

---

## 166.2 Cusp lemma: an outer direction atom has cubic detour cost

### Lemma 166.1

If `nu` has an atom of mass `w>0`, then

\[
\boxed{M-\pi\ge {w^3\over3M^2}.}
\tag{166.7}
\]

### Proof

For the `pi`-periodic kernel `g(t)=|cos t|`,

\[
\boxed{g''+g=2\delta_{\pi/2\pmod\pi}}
\tag{166.8}
\]

as distributions: near a zero, `|cos(pi/2+s)|=|sin s|`, whose first derivative jumps from `-1` to `+1`.

Therefore

\[
F''+F=2\widetilde\nu,
\]

where `widetilde nu` is `nu` shifted by `pi/2`. Since `F<=M`,

\[
h''=2\widetilde\nu-Fd\alpha\ge-Md\alpha.
\tag{166.9}
\]

Thus locally `h(alpha_0+t)+(M/2)t^2` is convex. At the shifted location of an atom of mass `w`, the derivative jump of `h` is exactly `2w`, so at least one outward one-sided derivative has magnitude at least `w`. Reflecting if needed, convexity and `h(alpha_0)>=0` give

\[
h(\alpha_0+t)\ge wt-{M\over2}t^2
\qquad(t\ge0).
\tag{166.10}
\]

Because `w<=M`, the interval `0<=t<=2w/M` has length at most `2<pi`, so it does not wrap around the projective circle. Integrating the positive parabola,

\[
\int h
\ge
\int_0^{2w/M}\left(wt-{M\over2}t^2\right)dt
={2w^3\over3M^2}.
\]

Equation (166.6) proves (166.7). `square`

The hostile recomputation confirms the factor `1/3`. Stopping at `w/M` would give the weaker `1/6` constant.

---

## 166.3 Train atom endpoint payment

Let the empirical stopped-train direction measures satisfy

\[
{T\over D}\to x,
\qquad
\mu_D\Rightarrow\mu.
\]

For an atom `phi` of `mu`, define its absolute train mass

\[
a=x\mu(\{\phi\}).
\tag{166.11}
\]

Every stopped train has two terminal face-side occurrences. For any projective neighborhood `U` of `phi`,

\[
2\#\{\tau:[u_\tau]\in U\}
\le
N_{out}(U)+(Q+4f).
\tag{166.12}
\]

Choose nested neighborhoods whose boundaries are null for both limiting measures, take the source limit, then shrink to `phi`. If `w=nu({phi})`,

\[
\boxed{w\ge(2a-B)_+.}
\tag{166.13}
\]

Lemma 166.1 yields, for every train-direction atom,

\[
\boxed{(2a-B)_+^3\le3M^2\delta,}
\tag{166.14}
\]

or equivalently

\[
\boxed{2a\le B+(3M^2\delta)^{1/3}.}
\tag{166.15}
\]

The occurrence convention is exact for singleton trains and bridges: both the endpoint multigraph and the outer walk count physical face-side occurrences.

---

## 166.4 Weighted Mantel forces a large train atom

Write the train atom masses in absolute `D` normalization as `a_i`, and put

\[
A=\sum_i a_i=x\sigma,
\qquad m=\sup_i a_i.
\tag{166.16}
\]

Partition the projective circle into the three-point orbits of `phi -> phi+pi/3 mod pi`. For orbit `r`, let `M_r` be its total atom mass and `E_r` its limiting strict-rhombus crossing mass. Audited §142 gives

\[
E_r\le M_r^2/4,
\qquad
\sum_rE_r=\Delta_2.
\tag{166.17}
\]

Every orbit contains at most three atom locations, so `M_r<=3m`. Therefore

\[
\Delta_2
\le{1\over4}\sum_rM_r^2
\le{3m\over4}\sum_rM_r
={3mA\over4}.
\tag{166.18}
\]

The §142 exposed-hull inequality `q/T<=2(1-sigma)` gives

\[
A=x\sigma\le x-{1\over2}\lim q/D=C.
\tag{166.19}
\]

Hence

\[
\boxed{m\ge{4\Delta_2\over3C}.}
\tag{166.20}
\]

Apply (166.15) to atoms approaching the supremum `m`. Then

\[
B+(3M^2\delta)^{1/3}
\ge2m
\ge{8\Delta_2\over3C},
\]

proving (166.3). Countably many atoms and infinitely many positive phase orbits cause no compactness issue here.

---

## 166.5 Scalar elimination and explicit low-capacity consequence

The outer ledger

\[
b+Q=2(q-k)
\]

and `q<=per(F)<=pi D` give

\[
M\le2\pi.
\tag{166.21}
\]

Also `T=q+2f-k`, so

\[
C={q\over2D}+{2f\over D}-{k\over D}
\le{\pi+B\over2},
\tag{166.22}
\]

because `B=(Q+4f)/D>=4f/D` and `k>=0`. These substitutions give (166.4).

Define

\[
B_0={\sqrt{\pi^2+32\pi/(3\sqrt3)}-\pi\over2}
=1.1318319134366228\ldots .
\tag{166.23}
\]

For any fixed `0<=beta<B_0`, if

\[
\limsup {Q+4f\over D}\le\beta,
\]

then the decreasing function

\[
g(B)={8\pi\over3\sqrt3(\pi+B)}-B
\]

and (166.4) give

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {b-\operatorname{per}(F)\over D}
\ge
{1\over12\pi^2}
\left(
{8\pi\over3\sqrt3(\pi+\beta)}-\beta
\right)^3>0.
}
\tag{166.24}
\]

At formal `beta=0`,

\[
\delta\ge0.030813662630724\ldots .
\tag{166.25}
\]

The zero-detour consequence `B>=B_0` is intentionally weaker than §154's sharper `B>=4sqrt(Delta_2)`. Section 166 gains scope, not endpoint sharpness: it remains quantitative for positive detour.

---

## 166.6 Audit boundaries and next obstruction

The hostile audit checked:

1. the distributional coefficient in `|cos|''+|cos|` and the `1/3` cubic constant;
2. semiconvex one-sided derivatives and the non-wrapping integration interval;
3. nested continuity neighborhoods for both train and outer measures;
4. exact singleton/bridge endpoint multiplicity;
5. weighted Mantel with countably many atoms and potentially infinitely many `pi/3` orbits;
6. use of a supremum rather than an assumed largest atom;
7. the directions of both scalar eliminations in (166.21)–(166.22).

No full-source affine-kernel extension is used; contact-isolated source points remain retained.

The theorem does not control total outer atomic mass: many small atoms can approximate Lebesgue direction measure while carrying little individual cubic cost. Thus §166 does not replace the exact zero-detour atomlessness theorem and does not yet close Type II. The next viable strengthening is to combine the all-regime §164 gap with orbit concentration or the exceptional-face direction-network constraints so that a larger fraction of atomic train mass is forced through finitely many outer atoms.

**Disposition:** audited-working proof-only quantitative bridge. C-II and Level I remain open.